> Yes—there are several issues to fix before relying on this for deployments.
> 
> ## 1. Problems in the current script
> 
> ### Paths and variables
> 
> ```bash
> LOCAL_PATH="~/projects/arachne"
> ```
> 
> Because `~` is inside quotes, Bash will **not** expand it to your home directory. Use:
> 
> ```bash
> LOCAL_PATH="$HOME/projects/arachne"
> ```
> 
> Also, these variables are referenced but never defined:
> 
> ```bash
> $LOCAL_LOGS
> $REMOTE_LOGS
> $PROJECT_PATH
> $date
> ```
> 
> Presumably `LOCAL_LOGS` / `REMOTE_LOGS` should be based on `LOCAL_PATH` / `REMOTE_PATH`.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Your SSH heredocs prevent variable substitution
> 
> This:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh existencia.org << 'ENDSSH'
>     cp -r ${REMOTE_PATH} ${BACKUP_PATH}
> ENDSSH
> ```
> 
> uses a *quoted* heredoc delimiter (`<< 'ENDSSH'`). That deliberately prevents local Bash from expanding `${REMOTE_PATH}` and `${BACKUP_PATH}`.
> 
> The remote shell also does not know those variables, so it will see empty values unless they happen to be defined in the remote shell environment.
> 
> You can either:
> 
> 1. let local variables expand (less robust for complex scripts), or
> 2. pass them as arguments to a remote Bash process (recommended).
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh existencia.org bash -s -- "$REMOTE_PATH" "$BACKUP_PATH" <<'REMOTE'
> remote_path=$1
> backup_path=$2
> 
> cp -a -- "$remote_path" "$backup_path"
> REMOTE
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Use absolute remote paths
> 
> This is risky:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_PATH="projects/arachne"
> ```
> 
> A relative remote path depends on the remote account’s working directory. Use an absolute path, such as:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_PATH="/home/YOUR_DREAMHOST_USER/projects/arachne"
> BACKUP_ROOT="/home/YOUR_DREAMHOST_USER/projects/arachne-backups"
> ```
> 
> Find your DreamHost home directory with:
> 
> ```bash
> ssh existencia.org 'pwd'
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Backup behavior is not safe yet
> 
> This:
> 
> ```bash
> cp -r ${REMOTE_PATH} ${BACKUP_PATH}
> ```
> 
> has a few problems:
> 
> - A backup destination that already exists can cause nested directories such as `arachne-backup/arachne`.
> - It overwrites or mixes backups rather than retaining deploy-specific versions.
> - It does not preserve metadata as well as `cp -a`.
> - Your rollback command copies the backup *into* the target rather than replacing the target.
> 
> Prefer timestamped backup directories:
> 
> ```bash
> DEPLOY_ID="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
> BACKUP_PATH="$BACKUP_ROOT/$DEPLOY_ID"
> ```
> 
> Then restore by removing the failed deployment directory and copying the backup back in its place.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### `git commit` fails when there is nothing to commit
> 
> With:
> 
> ```bash
> set -e
> git add . && git commit -m "Automated sync" && git push
> ```
> 
> the script stops when there are no staged changes, because `git commit` exits nonzero in that case.
> 
> Use:
> 
> ```bash
> git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" add -A
> 
> if ! git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" diff --cached --quiet; then
>     git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" commit -m "Automated deployment: $DEPLOY_ID"
>     git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" push
> else
>     echo "No code changes to commit."
> fi
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ### The error trap is declared too late
> 
> Your `trap ... ERR` is at the end of the script. It cannot catch errors that occur before Bash reaches that line.
> 
> Also, the trap’s rollback command refers to unrelated placeholder paths:
> 
> ```bash
> user@dreamhost
> /path/to/project
> ${BACKUP_PATH}project_backup_latest
> ```
> 
> Define the trap near the beginning, after the backup configuration is known.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Your health check uses an undefined date variable
> 
> This will not work:
> 
> ```bash
> if curl -s $UPDATE_CHECK_URL | grep -q "($date)"; then
> ```
> 
> `$date` is never set, and the parentheses are literal characters.
> 
> A much better pattern is:
> 
> 1. Generate a unique deployment ID locally.
> 2. Write that exact ID to a file on the server.
> 3. Make `/checkupdate` return that file’s contents.
> 4. Verify that the endpoint returns the expected ID.
> 
> For example:
> 
> ```bash
> DEPLOY_ID="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
> ```
> 
> Then:
> 
> ```bash
> curl --fail --silent --show-error "$UPDATE_CHECK_URL" |
>     grep -Fqx "$DEPLOY_ID"
> ```
> 
> This confirms that your web application is actually serving the version just deployed—not merely that the server responded.
> 
