> ## `checkupdate` endpoint
> 
> For this to work, `https://arachne.existencia.org/checkupdate` should return the literal contents of `last_updated.txt`, with no page template or extra text.
> 
> For example, in Flask:
> 
> ```python
> from pathlib import Path
> from flask import Response
> 
> @app.route("/checkupdate")
> def checkupdate():
>     deployment_id = Path("last_updated.txt").read_text().strip()
>     return Response(deployment_id + "\n", mimetype="text/plain")
> ```
> 
> The test is then meaningful: it checks that the public web app—not merely the SSH server—serves the exact deployment you just made.
> 
> If your application process retains the contents of the file in memory, have the endpoint read the file on every request, as above. If it fails because the old application process is still running, enable the relevant DreamHost/Passenger restart command in the remote deployment section.
> 
> ## Virtual environments and dependencies
> 
> A virtual environment is not something you generally synchronize by copying it between your Mac and DreamHost. It can contain platform-specific compiled packages and absolute paths:
> 
> - macOS packages may not run on DreamHost Linux;
> - the Python version may differ;
> - virtual environments encode their location;
> - native dependencies may need Linux-specific wheels or compilation.
> 
> Instead, synchronize a **declaration of dependencies**, then build the remote environment from it.
> 
> ### Simple option: `requirements.txt`
> 
> On your local machine, while your local environment is activated:
> 
> ```bash
> venv/bin/python -m pip freeze > requirements.txt
> ```
> 
> Review it, then commit it:
> 
> ```bash
> git add requirements.txt
> git commit -m "Record Python dependencies"
> git push
> ```
> 
